Zero is licensed by component. The rule: anything that runs inside WordPress
is GPL-2.0-or-later, because WordPress plugins are derivative works of
WordPress. Everything else is MIT.

GPL-2.0-or-later (full text in LICENSE-GPL)

  runtime/Zero/          the PHP runtime, shipped as the zero-runtime plugin
  runtime/identity/      the zero-identity plugin and its screens
  runtime/admin/         the zero-admin plugin and its screens, emitted
                         with `admin: true`
  runtime/wp-plugin/     the zero-runtime and zero-app plugin templates
  runtime/pages/         the optional pages runtime, copied into the
                         zero-pages-<app> plugin that zero-pages build and
                         zero compile-sources emit

  and every plugin that `zero build` emits from them.

MIT (full text in LICENSE-MIT)

  src/, build.rs, Cargo.toml, Cargo.lock   the Rust compiler and CLI
  runtime/client/                          the typed TypeScript client
  types/                                   the "zero" type declarations
  examples/, docs/, scripts/, tests/       examples, documentation, tooling, tests
  everything else not listed above

How the two meet. The compiler binary embeds the GPL runtime files as opaque
bytes and writes them into the plugins it emits, the runtime unchanged and the
plugin headers with their version placeholders filled in. It never links
against or derives from them; where a command needs the runtime, it runs
it as a separate PHP process. The runtime is to the compiler what
an installer's payload is to the installer: distributed together, but
separate works. The compiler's sources stay MIT, and because the packaged
crate carries the embedded runtime, Cargo.toml declares the package as
MIT AND GPL-2.0-or-later. The plugins written under
.zero/wp-content/plugins (zero-runtime, zero-identity, zero-app-<name>) are
GPL-2.0-or-later and each carries the license text. Files the compiler
generates from your zero.config.ts (schema.php, functions.php, migrations,
zero.gen.ts, zero/client/types.ts and schema.ts) are yours. The client's
index.ts and README.md are copied from runtime/client/ and stay MIT; the
copy ships with its LICENSE.

Third-party code keeps its own license and is not relicensed by this file:

  runtime/Zero/vendor-prefixed/            WordPress PHP toolkit (GPL-2.0-or-later)
                                           and the libraries it bundles
  runtime/identity/vendor-prefixed/        passkey, JWT and TOTP libraries
  runtime/identity/build/                  the bundled identity screens
  runtime/admin/build/                     the bundled admin screens

  Each of those directories carries a THIRD-PARTY-LICENSES.txt listing every
  package and its license. Rust dependencies are under their own permissive
  licenses, recorded in each crate's metadata and license files (Cargo.lock
  names the crates and versions).
